.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_PEER_CERT_VERIFIED 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified \- report whether the TLS client presented a verified certificate
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

bool chttpsvr_req_peer_cert_verified(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns true only when the request arrived over TLS, the client
presented a certificate during the handshake, and that certificate
verified against the ca_bundle_path of the server.

It returns false for a plaintext connection, for a server with no
ca_bundle_path, for a client that presented no certificate, and when
req is NULL.

With the default configuration, a server that has a ca_bundle_path
accepts no client without such a certificate, so every request there
reports true.  When client_cert_optional is set in chttp_tls_config_t,
a client that presents nothing is accepted as well, and this function
is how a handler or a middleware tells such a client apart from a
verified one.  In either mode, a client whose certificate does not
verify never reaches a handler.

The answer belongs to the connection, so every request on one
keep-alive connection reports the same value, and a resumed TLS
session reports the answer of the handshake that established it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true or false, as described above.
.fi
